{
  "item_id": "PRISMA_2020_3",
  "elements": [
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e1",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_4",
      "quote": "Since the onset of Coronavirus Disease 2019 (COVID19), SARS-CoV-2 infection has been circulating globally, with approximately six peak waves of outbreaks worldwide before May 26, 2023, with a cumulative number of confirmed SARS-CoV-2 infections of 76,689,575 and a total number of deaths of 6,935,889 [1], posing a serious threat to the health of populations in all countries. ... The fact that SARS-CoV-2 is still mutating indicates that it remains a global health threat. The WHO study published in Nature [1, 3] also suggested that accurately tracking SARS-CoV-2 and its impact had been challenging.",
      "reason": "The introduction describes the global state of SARS-CoV-2 spread, ongoing mutation, and challenges/uncertainties in tracking impact, which sets out current knowledge and uncertainties."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e2",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_2",
      "quote": "Background There is a significant increase in the number of SARS-CoV-2 reinfection reports in various countries. However, the trend of reinfection rate over time is not clear. ... The results of this study could help guide public health measures and vaccination strategies in response to the Coronavirus Disease 2019 (COVID-19) pandemic.",
      "reason": "The abstract states that trends in reinfection rates are unclear and explains the study's importance for guiding public health measures and vaccination strategies, justifying the need for the review."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e3",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_15",
      "quote": "The search strategy we used was based on previous studies, and the process of literature screening and information extraction strictly followed PRISMA standards. In addition, the advantage of this study was that compared with the previous meta-analysis of the SARSCoV-2 reinfection rate, first, to avoid the heterogeneous impact of SARS-CoV-2 reinfection definition on SARSCoV-2 reinfection rates, so this study defined reinfection and included only literature with reinfection intervals greater than 90 days. Secondly, considering the heterogeneity of reinfection rates of different variants, we constructed a meta-regression of time-varying reinfection rates and compared it with the temporal spline regression without considering heterogeneity to explore the temporal trends of SARS-CoV-2 reinfection rates, which could provide a basis for subsequent research on prediction of SARS-CoV-2 infection, and could guide significance for the future policy formulation of SARS-CoV-2 reinfection.",
      "reason": "The discussion explicitly compares this study to previous meta-analyses and explains how the current review differs and adds value, justifying the need for this review relative to prior work."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e4",
      "status": "NOT_APPLICABLE",
      "chunk_id": null,
      "quote": null,
      "reason": "The review assesses reinfection rates (observational epidemiological outcomes) rather than effects of interventions; no intervention mechanism of action is proposed or relevant in the manuscript."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e5",
      "status": "NOT_APPLICABLE",
      "chunk_id": null,
      "quote": null,
      "reason": "The review is not of a complex intervention or delivery context and no logic model is presented or required for this epidemiological meta-analysis of reinfection rates."
    }
  ],
  "confidence": 90
}

{
  "item_id": "PRISMA_2020_4",
  "elements": [
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_4_e1",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_7",
      "quote": "Therefore, the aim of this study was to conduct a meta-analysis to describe and summarize the current reinfection rates in different regions and among diverse populations. Furthermore, the study sought to examine trends in reinfection rates over time, so as to provide reference for the development of global unified reinfection standards and reinfection measures in the future.",
      "reason": "The manuscript explicitly states the study aims/objectives, including describing and summarizing reinfection rates across regions/populations and examining time trends, which directly answers the requirement to provide explicit objectives/questions."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_4_e2",
      "status": "NOT_APPLICABLE",
      "chunk_id": "text_6",
      "quote": "A meta-regression analysis was conducted on literature reporting cases of SARS-CoV-2 reinfection globally to estimate the incidence of reinfection of SARS-CoV-2 (as of March 16, 2023), and reported according to the Preferred Reporting Items for Systematic Reviews and MetaAnalyses (PRISMA) guidelines (Additional file 1).",
      "reason": "This review is an observational incidence/meta-regression study of reinfection rates rather than a review of intervention effects; therefore PICO-style planned comparisons for interventions do not apply."
    }
  ],
  "confidence": 90
}

{
  "item_id": "PRISMA_2020_5",
  "elements": [
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e1",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_7",
      "quote": "The inclusion criteria were: (1) Conformed to research questions, i.e., studies that estimated the reinfection rate of SARS-CoV-2 or we could calculate it based on the data provided in the paper; (2) The study design included cohort study, case-control study, and descriptive study; (3) The study fited our working definition of SARS-CoV-2 reinfection. ... reinfection working definition in this study was based on a positive laboratory result at least 90 days after laboratory confirmation of primary infection(...). (4) Original research. The exclusion criteria were: (1) The sample size was less than 20; (2) Belonged to one of the following types of studies: editorial, case report, case series study, systematic review, meta-analysis, animal experiment, news report; (3) Secondary reporting or articles on repeated studies of the same population; (4) Grey literature, including a range of documents not controlled by commercial publishing organizations.",
      "reason": "The text explicitly lists study-level inclusion/exclusion criteria, eligible study designs (cohort, case-control, descriptive), sample size threshold, original research requirement, and the working definition of reinfection (>=90 days), which are study characteristics used to determine eligibility."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e2",
      "status": "PARTIAL",
      "chunk_id": "text_7",
      "quote": "To avoid language bias, the search was not limited to any language. We conducted preliminary screening based on the English title and English abstract provided by nonEnglish articles. Further, in the screening process, if the included article was neither in English nor in Chinese and did not provide an English abstract or full text, we used an online translator (Google Translate) for translation. There were no non-English articles in the final articles included in this study. ... The exclusion criteria were: ... (4) Grey literature, including a range of documents not controlled by commercial publishing organizations.",
      "reason": "The review specifies language handling (no language limits, translation approach) and exclusion of grey literature (report status). However, dissemination year or other report-date restrictions are not specified in the provided evidence."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e3",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_9",
      "quote": "Reports assessed for eligibility (n = 206) ... Reports excluded: unclear reinfection definition /sample size<20/Unclear data/wrong study design/the number of first infections was not reported(n = 128) Mathematical models (n = 23)",
      "reason": "The PRISMA flowchart (figure) and accompanying text distinguish exclusions for unclear data or missing reported counts (e.g., 'the number of first infections was not reported') separately from other reasons such as unclear definition or wrong study design, indicating a distinction between outcomes not measured/reported."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e4",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_10",
      "quote": "Subgroup analyses were performed for age, reinfection variant, country, study population, and study type respectively, and the results were shown in Table 1, and forest plots for each subgroup analysis were shown in Additional file 6.",
      "reason": "The review specifies the groups used in syntheses (age groups, variants, country, study population, study type) and links these subgroup analyses to comparisons reported in results and Table 1."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_5_e5",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_7",
      "quote": "Therefore, the aim of this study was to conduct a meta-analysis to describe and summarize the current reinfection rates in different regions and among diverse populations. Furthermore, the study sought to examine trends in reinfection rates over time... reinfection working definition in this study was based on a positive laboratory result at least 90 days after laboratory confirmation of primary infection(...). In order to avoid data heterogeneity in metaanalysis while ensuring data quantity, we used previous literature and previous definitions of reinfection in some countries as the criteria for included literature and data extraction in this study.",
      "reason": "The authors provide a rationale for using a >=90-day reinfection definition (to avoid heterogeneity while ensuring data quantity) and note exclusion of grey literature and small sample sizes, giving reasons for notable eligibility restrictions."
    }
  ],
  "confidence": "85"
}

